1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,605
Welcome to Module 6.

2
00:00:02,955 --> 00:00:06,735
Your agents can now think
in a loop and use tools safely.

3
00:00:07,085 --> 00:00:10,046
But close the program,
and they forget everything.

4
00:00:10,396 --> 00:00:14,410
Even inside one chat, they
only remember what you send them.

5
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:20,741
By the end of this module, you'll build
a persistent study companion: it remembers

6
00:00:20,741 --> 00:00:26,795
your name, your goals and what you find
hard, across days, in a real database, with

7
00:00:26,795 --> 00:00:28,034
privacy controls.

8
00:00:29,834 --> 00:00:34,210
In this lesson, you'll learn
what stateless means for a model API.

9
00:00:34,810 --> 00:00:39,008
You'll prove it, by asking the
same question with and without history.

10
00:00:39,608 --> 00:00:42,380
You'll build a chat loop
that keeps the conversation.

11
00:00:42,980 --> 00:00:46,461
And you'll meet the four
kinds of memory an agent can have.

12
00:00:48,261 --> 00:00:50,446
Let's start with an everyday picture.

13
00:00:50,796 --> 00:00:54,354
Imagine a brilliant
tutor, with one strange problem.

14
00:00:54,704 --> 00:00:58,174
The moment you leave the
room, they forget you completely.

15
00:00:58,524 --> 00:01:00,979
Your name, your last lesson, everything.

16
00:01:01,329 --> 00:01:03,052
You can still get great help.

17
00:01:03,402 --> 00:01:08,161
You just have to bring your notebook
every time, and show them what you covered

18
00:01:08,161 --> 00:01:08,583
before.

19
00:01:09,183 --> 00:01:10,790
The tutor is the model.

20
00:01:11,140 --> 00:01:14,562
Very smart, but it
remembers nothing between visits.

21
00:01:15,162 --> 00:01:19,585
The notebook is the list of
messages you send with every request.

22
00:01:20,185 --> 00:01:24,670
And carrying the notebook is
your code's job, on every single call.

23
00:01:25,270 --> 00:01:26,918
That's what stateless means.

24
00:01:27,268 --> 00:01:30,073
Each request to the API stands alone.

25
00:01:30,423 --> 00:01:33,830
The server doesn't keep
your conversation between calls.

26
00:01:34,180 --> 00:01:39,075
If the model seems to remember, it's
because your code sent the history again.

27
00:01:40,875 --> 00:01:42,351
Let's prove it with code.

28
00:01:42,701 --> 00:01:46,430
The code is on the left, and
what it printed is on the right.

29
00:01:47,030 --> 00:01:50,368
As in every module, no API key is needed.

30
00:01:50,718 --> 00:01:55,195
A scripted fake plays the model,
and the first output line says so.

31
00:01:55,545 --> 00:02:01,110
The important thing: like a real
model, the fake only knows what is inside the

32
00:02:01,110 --> 00:02:01,680
request.

33
00:02:02,280 --> 00:02:07,811
This small function sends a list of
messages, and returns the text of the reply.

34
00:02:08,411 --> 00:02:09,206
Call one.

35
00:02:09,556 --> 00:02:11,752
We say: hi, my name is Anuj.

36
00:02:12,352 --> 00:02:17,392
Call two is a brand new request,
with only the question: what's my name?

37
00:02:17,742 --> 00:02:19,798
And the answer is: I don't know.

38
00:02:20,148 --> 00:02:21,927
Nothing in this request says it.

39
00:02:22,277 --> 00:02:23,497
Call one is gone.

40
00:02:24,097 --> 00:02:29,681
Call three sends the earlier messages
again: our introduction, the reply, and then

41
00:02:29,681 --> 00:02:30,566
the question.

42
00:02:30,916 --> 00:02:33,843
Now the answer is: your name is Anuj.

43
00:02:34,193 --> 00:02:35,943
Same model, same question.

44
00:02:36,293 --> 00:02:38,376
The only difference is what we sent.

45
00:02:40,176 --> 00:02:42,600
Now, how does a normal chat work, then?

46
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:46,429
The first request has
one message: the user's.

47
00:02:46,779 --> 00:02:48,947
The API answers and forgets.

48
00:02:49,547 --> 00:02:55,249
For the second request, your code sends
three messages: the first user message, the

49
00:02:55,249 --> 00:02:58,271
assistant's reply,
and the new user message.

50
00:02:58,871 --> 00:03:00,850
The third request sends five.

51
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:06,852
The conversation grows by two messages
every turn, and the whole thing travels with

52
00:03:06,852 --> 00:03:07,805
every request.

53
00:03:08,155 --> 00:03:13,345
Keep this picture in mind: it's the
reason for everything in the next two lessons.

54
00:03:15,145 --> 00:03:16,732
Here's a real chat loop.

55
00:03:17,082 --> 00:03:20,464
One list, history,
holds the whole conversation.

56
00:03:21,064 --> 00:03:25,389
Each line of input becomes a
user message, appended to the list.

57
00:03:25,739 --> 00:03:30,089
For a repeatable demo, the three
lines come from a small text file.

58
00:03:30,689 --> 00:03:33,025
Then we send the whole list, every time.

59
00:03:33,625 --> 00:03:36,596
And we append the reply
as an assistant message.

60
00:03:36,946 --> 00:03:41,580
Forget this line, and the model
never sees its own earlier answers.

61
00:03:42,180 --> 00:03:46,425
Turn one: two messages stored,
and thirteen input tokens sent.

62
00:03:47,025 --> 00:03:48,632
Turn two: what's my name?

63
00:03:48,982 --> 00:03:51,765
Anuj, because the
history went with the request.

64
00:03:52,365 --> 00:03:56,453
And look at the input
tokens: thirteen, sixty-one, ninety.

65
00:03:56,803 --> 00:03:58,290
Every turn sends more.

66
00:03:58,640 --> 00:04:04,108
You pay for input tokens on every call,
and the model has a maximum it can read.

67
00:04:04,458 --> 00:04:07,524
That's the problem we
solve in lessons two and three.

68
00:04:09,324 --> 00:04:12,403
Let's name the kinds of
memory an agent can have.

69
00:04:12,753 --> 00:04:16,130
First, conversation
history: the messages list.

70
00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:18,586
Exact, but it grows every turn.

71
00:04:19,186 --> 00:04:23,312
Second, a short-term window:
send only the last few turns.

72
00:04:23,662 --> 00:04:25,826
Cheap, but old details fall out.

73
00:04:26,426 --> 00:04:32,041
Third, summary memory: old turns
compressed into a short summary that keeps the

74
00:04:32,041 --> 00:04:32,396
gist.

75
00:04:32,996 --> 00:04:38,885
And fourth, long-term memory: facts
saved in a database, which survive a restart,

76
00:04:38,885 --> 00:04:41,357
and a brand new chat the next day.

77
00:04:43,157 --> 00:04:44,624
Where does each one live?

78
00:04:44,974 --> 00:04:49,705
History lives in a Python list,
and it's lost when the program exits.

79
00:04:50,305 --> 00:04:53,085
The window is just the
part of the list you send.

80
00:04:53,435 --> 00:04:56,383
A turn is lost from the
request when it slides out.

81
00:04:56,983 --> 00:05:00,376
The summary is a string,
sent with the system prompt.

82
00:05:00,726 --> 00:05:04,121
Details are lost if they
don't make it into the summary.

83
00:05:04,721 --> 00:05:08,242
And long-term memory
lives in a database file.

84
00:05:08,592 --> 00:05:11,460
It's only lost when
you delete it, on purpose.

85
00:05:13,260 --> 00:05:15,099
Pause the video and try this.

86
00:05:15,449 --> 00:05:19,072
The history so far is:
user says, I love chess.

87
00:05:19,422 --> 00:05:20,960
Assistant says, nice.

88
00:05:21,310 --> 00:05:25,450
Then your code sends a new
request with only: what do I love?

89
00:05:26,050 --> 00:05:28,127
What can the model answer, and why?

90
00:05:28,477 --> 00:05:30,322
And what would you change in the code?

91
00:05:36,922 --> 00:05:37,955
It can't know.

92
00:05:38,305 --> 00:05:43,476
The request doesn't mention chess,
and the API keeps nothing between calls.

93
00:05:43,826 --> 00:05:49,131
The fix: append every message to the
history list, and send the whole list each

94
00:05:49,131 --> 00:05:49,467
time.

95
00:05:51,267 --> 00:05:52,408
Interview corner.

96
00:05:52,758 --> 00:05:56,297
Does the Claude API
remember earlier messages?

97
00:05:58,897 --> 00:06:00,327
A good answer: no.

98
00:06:00,677 --> 00:06:02,846
The Messages API is stateless.

99
00:06:03,196 --> 00:06:04,948
Each request stands alone.

100
00:06:05,298 --> 00:06:11,027
A conversation works because your code
resends the history, the user and assistant

101
00:06:11,027 --> 00:06:13,682
messages in order, with every request.

102
00:06:14,032 --> 00:06:16,890
The trap is saying the
model remembers your chat.

103
00:06:17,240 --> 00:06:20,420
Any memory is data
your code stores, and sends.

104
00:06:21,020 --> 00:06:24,702
A follow-up: why not just
keep the whole history forever?

105
00:06:27,302 --> 00:06:28,992
Because of cost and limits.

106
00:06:29,342 --> 00:06:35,101
Every turn resends everything, so input
tokens, and the time to answer, grow with

107
00:06:35,101 --> 00:06:36,310
the conversation.

108
00:06:36,660 --> 00:06:39,920
And eventually, it
won't fit the context window.

109
00:06:40,270 --> 00:06:44,644
So you need a strategy:
windows, summaries, and long-term facts.

110
00:06:44,994 --> 00:06:47,494
The trap is thinking
it's only about money.

111
00:06:47,844 --> 00:06:50,873
Long contexts also
make every reply slower.

112
00:06:52,673 --> 00:06:53,532
Quick check.

113
00:06:53,882 --> 00:06:57,752
Your chatbot forgets what
the user said two messages ago.

114
00:06:58,102 --> 00:06:59,752
What's the most likely bug?

115
00:07:05,952 --> 00:07:09,642
The code doesn't append every
message to the history it sends.

116
00:07:09,992 --> 00:07:12,556
The model only sees what's in the request.

117
00:07:14,356 --> 00:07:15,446
Let's recap.

118
00:07:15,796 --> 00:07:17,447
The API is stateless.

119
00:07:17,797 --> 00:07:19,548
Each request stands alone.

120
00:07:20,148 --> 00:07:24,159
A conversation is your code
resending the history, every time.

121
00:07:24,759 --> 00:07:27,411
Append the user's message, and the reply.

122
00:07:28,011 --> 00:07:33,245
And the history grows every turn,
which costs money and time, and hits limits.

123
00:07:33,845 --> 00:07:35,974
Next lesson: short-term memory.

124
00:07:36,324 --> 00:07:41,984
You'll measure how fast the
conversation grows, meet the context window, and keep

125
00:07:41,984 --> 00:07:45,897
only the turns that matter,
without breaking tool calls.
